[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 29, "caption": "Figure 1: C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomics and natural language for scalable single-cell analysis. A , The C2S-Scale corpus integrates over 50 million single-cell transcriptomes curated from the CELLxGENE and Human Cell Atlas repositories, linking transcriptomic profiles with rich cell, tissue, and donor-level metadata as well as scientific abstracts and papers. B , C2S-Scale expands upon the Cell2Sentence framework by increasing model capacity up to 27 billion parameters, building a multi-task training corpus of over 1 trillion tokens, and expanding task diversity to include multimodal and multicellular inputs. C , Transcriptomic profiles are transformed into 'cell sentences', ordered sequences of gene names ranked by expression level. This enables the direct application of pretrained LLMs without requiring architectural modifications. D , C2S-Scale is initialized from pre-trained LLM checkpoints. An initial training phase on cell sentences is followed by instruction fine-tuning for a variety of downstream tasks. E , This unified training regime enables broad downstream capabilities, ranging from standard cell annotation to complex tasks like perturbation prediction and biological question answering.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific schematic describing a cell/language foundation model pipeline.\n\nPanel A, “DATA SOURCES AND ANNOTATIONS,” shows five input/source categories with icons and scale labels:\n- Single-cell: “800+ datasets”\n- Cell clusters: “170M+ single-cell & bulk samples”\n- Tissue sections: “100M+ annotations”\n- Paper abstracts: “21M+ perturbations”\n- Donor metadata: “1B+ tokens”\n\nPanel B, “SCALING DIMENSIONS,” shows four boxed scaling factors:\n- Model Capacity: line plot with “Scaling law” and network icons\n- Dataset Size: “170M+ cells from over 800 datasets from CellxGene and Human Cell Atlas”\n- Multimodality: transcriptomics, perturbations, biological papers, donor metadata\n- Context Diversity: unified text interface for single-cell, multi-cell, and natural language input\n\nPanel C, “CELL SENTENCE REPRESENTATION,” shows a gene expression matrix with rows Cell 1-4 and columns genes A-F, transformed into textual “Cell Sentences,” e.g. gene-token sequences like “GeneE GeneB GeneD ...”.\n\nPanel D, “MODEL TRAINING PROCEDURE,” shows a three-stage training workflow:\n- Stage 1: Natural language pre-training using paper/document inputs into a Base LLM labeled “Pythia, Gemma-2”\n- Stage 2: Continued training on cell sentences, producing “C2S-Scale”\n- Stage 3: Instruction fine-tuning for downstream tasks, producing “Fine-tuned C2S-Scale”\nIt shows checkpoints between the base language model and cell-to-sentence model.\n\nPanel E, “DOWNSTREAM TASKS,” lists applications with example prompts/outputs:\n- Cell Type Annotation\n- Cell Generation\n- Perturbation Response Prediction\n- Cluster Captioning\n- Dataset Interpretation\n- Question Answering\n\nBiological source objects visible include single cells, cell clusters, tissue sections, gene expression matrices, perturbation/drug icons, transcriptomics/DNA icon, donor metadata, and biological paper abstracts. The central transformation is converting gene expression profiles into ordered gene-token “cell sentences” for language-model training and downstream biological prediction or interpretation tasks."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 30, "caption": "Figure 2: Caption continued on following page.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific benchmark figure with panels **A-E** comparing single-cell foundation/model performance.\n\nPanel **A**: Large dot-matrix and bar-summary benchmark table. Rows include models such as **C2S (Best)**, **scGPT**, **Geneformer**, **scFoundation**, **Gemini-1.5**, **GPT-4o**, **Linear classifier**, **PCA**, **scVI**, and **CellTypist**. Columns are grouped into tasks: **Cell Type Annotation**, **Cell Embedding**, and **Generative**. Individual task labels include immune, pancreas, lung, OOD tasks, integration, perturbation, and other abbreviated generative tasks. Blue circles encode performance from 0-1, orange rings mark best performer, and gray dots indicate incapable/N/A. Right-side horizontal bars summarize **Predictive**, **Generative**, and **Overall** scores.\n\nPanel **B**: Scatter/bubble plot titled **Compute scaling laws**. X-axis shows **Training Compute (log10 PetaFLOP days)**; y-axis shows **BERTopic Score (%)** with a secondary right axis for **Expressed Gene Overlap (%)**. Bubble size represents model scale, labeled from **410M** to **27B**. Points compare C2S variants including **C2S 410M**, **C2S 1B**, **C2S 2B**, and **C2S 27B** across tasks including **Cell Type Annotation**, **Dataset Interpretation**, and **Sample Generation**.\n\nPanel **C**: Scatter/bubble plot titled **OOD cell type annotation**. X-axis is training compute; y-axis is **OOD Annotation Accuracy**. It compares models such as **PCA**, **CellTypist**, **Linear classifier**, **scVI**, **Geneformer**, **C2S-1B**, **scGPT**, and **C2S-27B**. Marker color distinguishes model type: foundation versus simpler models. Larger C2S models appear in the higher-accuracy region.\n\nPanel **D**: Two stacked scatter/bubble plots for **LoRA fine-tuning**, with separate plots for **27B base** and **2B base**. X-axis is training compute; y-axis is **Cell Type BERTopic Score (%)**, with a right-side axis for **Dataset Interpretation BERTopic Score (%)**. Points are labeled by LoRA rank values such as **r=4**, **r=8**, **r=16**, **r=32**, **r=64**, and **r=128**.\n\nPanel **E**: Line plot titled **Sample efficiency scaling**. X-axis is **Training Samples Seen (million)**. Left y-axis is **BERTopic Score (%)** and right y-axis is **Expressed Gene Overlap (%)**. Multiple curves track cell type annotation, tissue annotation, multiple-cell type/sample annotation, dataset interpretation, and sample generation from tissue.\n\nBiological source objects visible in the figure are single-cell transcriptomic/cell-type related datasets and tasks, including immune, pancreas, lung, out-of-distribution cell type annotation, cell embeddings, perturbation, and sample/gene-expression generation. The figure presents transformations from biological single-cell data into annotation, embedding, interpretation, and generation benchmarks, evaluated through model interfaces such as foundation models, LLMs, linear classifiers, PCA, scVI, and CellTypist. Main finding shown visually: **C2S, especially larger variants, is repeatedly marked as best or near-best across predictive, embedding, generative, scaling, OOD annotation, LoRA tuning, and sample-efficiency evaluations.**"]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 32, "caption": "Figure 3: C2S-Scale enables natural language interpretation across biological hierarchies, from single cells to entire datasets. A , Overview of different scales of biological data interpretation, from single cells to cluster and datasetlevel annotation. B , Single-cell annotation performance on a diverse immune cell atlas [21]. The correspondence between ground truth and predicted labels demonstrates accurate annotation at the single-cell level. C , Cell annotation performance of C2S-Scale versus foundation model and simple model baselines in an in-distribution versus out-ofdistribution setting. C2S-Scale exhibits the smallest performance degradation when transferring from in-distribution (ID) to out-of-distribution (OOD) immune cell contexts. D , Cluster captioning performance on unseen data clusters. Models are tasked with generating descriptive biological captions given multi-cell context, with quality measured by BioBERTScore. E-F , Dataset-level summarization performance. Models generate natural language abstracts for entire scRNA-seq experiments, evaluated on held-out cells from seen studies (E) and completely unseen studies (F). Error bars represent standard deviation across test set samples.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about multi-scale biological interpretation of single-cell data.\n\nPanel A: Schematic titled “Multi-scale biological interpretation.” Shows UMAP-style cell embedding plots at three levels: “Single Cell,” “Cell Cluster,” and “Dataset / Tissue.” Each plot has axes labeled UMAP 1 and UMAP 2. A highlighted single point, circled cluster, or blue-tinted tissue/dataset region is connected to a chat-style model interface. Example prompts include “What cell type is this?”, “Describe this cluster.” and “Summarize this dataset.” Example model outputs identify a T cell, describe alveolar cells expressing CD3E and TRBC2 with active TCR signaling, and summarize immune cells from a healthy donor containing T cells, B cells, and monocytes.\n\nPanel B: Heatmap/confusion matrix comparing true cell types against predicted cell types. Labels include immune-cell categories such as CD16-/CD56+ NK, CD16+/CD56- NK, CD4+ alpha-beta T, CD8+ alpha-beta T, memory CD4/CD8 T, mucosal T, naive B, naive CD4 T, naive CD8 T, plasma cell, and plasmacytoid dendritic cells. Color scale indicates prediction accuracy from low to high.\n\nPanel C: Bar chart titled “ID vs OOD Evaluation Setup.” Top schematic shows train/test splits across Data 1 and Data 2 for in-distribution and out-of-distribution evaluation. Bottom compares accuracy for models including C2S-Scale-2B, scGPT, Geneformer, scFoundation, scVI, PCA, CellTypist, and Linear classifier. Solid bars indicate in-distribution accuracy and hatched bars indicate out-of-distribution accuracy.\n\nPanel D: Bar chart titled “Cluster Captioning,” showing BERTScore across models, including C2S-Scale-410M, C2S-Scale-1B, C2S-Scale-2B, CensusGPT, Llama-3.1-8B, InternVL-3B, Molmo-7B, Gemini 1.5 Flash, Qwen-14B, and GPT-4o.\n\nPanel E: Bar chart titled “Dataset Interpretation (ID),” reporting BERTScore for the same or similar models under in-distribution evaluation.\n\nPanel F: Bar chart titled “Dataset Interpretation (OOD),” reporting BERTScore for out-of-distribution dataset interpretation.\n\nVisible finding: C2S-Scale models, especially larger variants, appear competitive or strongest across cell type prediction, cluster captioning, and dataset interpretation, with higher reported ID and OOD scores than several baseline biological foundation models and general vision/language models."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 33, "caption": "Figure 4: C2S-Scale can interpret multi-cellular spatial context and predict niche neighborhoods. A, We fine-tune C2S-Scale on a variety of single and multi-cellular spatial tasks designed to enable C2S-Scale to perform spatial reasoning, including identifying whether cells belong to the same neighborhood, generating spatial neighbors, and predicting the niche label of a cell or group of cells. A 'neighborhood' is defined to be cells within a fixed radius from a central cell. B, C2S outperforms Nicheformer, scGPT, and other models in spatial neighborhood prediction accuracy (* = P < 0.05, ** = P < 0.01; McNemar's test). Dashed line represents random baseline accuracy (0.51). C, We use publicly available gene interaction databases including BioGRID and CellPhoneDB to construct natural language interaction prompts about gene interactions. Integrating gene interactions from BioGRID and CellPhoneDB individually improves performance, and their combination provides the greatest improvement (Panel B). These results highlight the multi-task transfer learning potential of C2S-Scale for spatially-aware biological modeling.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about spatial/neighborhood modeling in biology.\n\nPanel A: Schematic of spatial cell neighborhoods. Colored circular cells (teal, pink, blue/gray) are arranged in 2D space. A dashed circular radius labeled `r` defines a local neighborhood around a central teal cell. To the right are four task diagrams:\n- Task 1: Neighborhood Prediction: cells inside a dashed neighborhood are input, with output text indicating whether cells are in the same neighborhood.\n- Task 2: Neighborhood Generation: a neighborhood of multiple colored cells is used to generate/predict another teal cell.\n- Task 3: Niche label prediction: individual colored cell examples are mapped to labels such as “Tumor niche” and “Zone 2b niche.”\n- Task 4: Same niche prediction: a group of colored cells is mapped to an answer stating whether cells belong to different niches.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart titled “Spatial Neighborhood Prediction.” Y-axis is “Spatial Prediction Accuracy.” Methods on the x-axis include `scGPT`, `Nicheformer`, `Linear Model`, `GITIII`, `CZ`, `CZs w/ CPDB`, `CZs w/ BIOGRID`, and `CZs w/ BIOGRID & CPDB`. Bars increase from lower accuracy for baseline methods to the highest accuracy for `CZs w/ BIOGRID & CPDB`. A dashed horizontal reference line is shown near 0.5, with significance brackets marked by asterisks above comparisons.\n\nPanel C: Schematic of input biological data sources and a model/question interface. Left shows a small gene expression matrix with rows labeled Cell 1 through Cell 4 and columns labeled genes A through F. Middle shows “Interaction Databases (CellPhoneDB, BioGRID)” with a diagram of two interacting cells connected by arrows/interaction lines. Right shows a text query box asking: “What gene codes for proteins that interact with the protein coded by GeneA in the extracellular matrix?” The answer shown is “GeneB.”\n\nBiological source objects visible: spatially arranged cells, gene expression matrix, gene identifiers, cell-cell/protein interaction databases, extracellular-matrix protein interaction query. The figure presents transformations from spatial cell neighborhoods and omics/interaction data into prediction, generation, niche classification, and question-answering/model interface outputs."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 33, "caption": "Figure 5: C2S-Scale demonstrates superior single-cell question answering performance compared to state-of-the-art (SOTA) LLMs. A, Example QA scenario based on scRNA-seq data. B, Empirical comparison of C2S-Scale and SOTA LLMs on single-cell QA tasks, highlighting C2S-Scale's advantage in domain-specific reasoning. Error bars represent standard deviation across test set QA samples. C, Overview of the GRPO framework, which further refines model performance by training on preference data.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **A**, **B**, and **C**.\n\n**Panel A:** Schematic prompt-style example for single-cell RNA-seq interpretation. It shows a boxed **Context** statement about inhibitory neurons in the thalamus being identifiable by genes involved in GABA synthesis and neurotransmission. A second box labeled **scRNA-seq Data** contains a “Cell sentence: Gene A Gene X Gene Y …” with a blue abstract cell/gene-expression-like illustration. A **Question** box asks whether the given cell is an inhibitory neuron. An **Answer** box states “Yes,” citing **GAD1** and **GAD2** expression as evidence for GABA production and inhibitory neuron identity. A **C2S** logo appears at lower left.\n\n**Panel B:** Bar chart comparing **BioBERT Score** across models. Blue bars correspond to C2S-scale model variants, including **C2S-Scale 410M SFT**, **C2S-Scale 410M GRPO**, **C2S-Scale 1B SFT**, and **C2S-Scale 1B GRPO**. Gray bars compare other models including **Llama-3.2 3B**, **Llama-3.1 8B**, **BioMistral 7B**, **Gemini 1.5 Flash**, **GPT-4o-mini**, and **GPT-4o**. Error bars are shown. The C2S-scale models have visibly higher BioBERT scores than the gray baseline models.\n\n**Panel C:** Workflow diagram for **Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)**. A C2S model/logo receives or generates multiple response outputs labeled **r1**, **r2**, and **r3**, which are scored into reward/advantage boxes **A1**, **A2**, and **A3**. A formula is shown for advantage normalization: approximately `A_i = (r_i - mean(r)) / std(r)`. The diagram indicates grouped relative scoring used for policy optimization.\n\nVisible biological source objects include scRNA-seq gene-expression data represented as a “cell sentence” and inhibitory neuron marker genes **GAD1** and **GAD2**. The figure combines a biological question-answering example, model performance comparison, and reinforcement-learning optimization interface/workflow."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 34, "caption": "Figure 6: C2S-Scale models outperform existing methods in predicting cellular responses to unseen perturbations. A, Overview of the C2S-Scale perturbation prediction framework, which supports diverse perturbation types including drugs, cytokines, and genetic knockouts, utilizing Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to prioritize the accurate reconstruction of key gene programs such as apoptosis or interferon response. B, UMAPs comparing predicted vs. ground-truth responses for unseen perturbations across four models. Rows show: (1) all combinatorial perturbations, (2) CD4 T cells under IFNγ , (3) B cells under the held-out IFNβ + IL-6 stimulation. C2S-Scale aligns closely with ground truth in all cases. C, Benchmark metrics show C2S-Scale outperforms scGen, CellOT, scGPT, CondOT, BioLord, and linear models across all evaluation criteria. D, GRPO improves over SFT on L1000 (apoptosis response) and cytokine stimulation (interferon response) tasks, with gains in Kendall's τ , Pearson's r , and scFID. E, Benchmarking on the SciPlex3 dataset for unseen small molecule perturbations. C2S-Scale outperforms ChemCPA, scGPT, and CondOT across scFID, MMD, and Wasserstein metrics, demonstrating the model's ability to leverage pathway information for out-of-distribution prediction.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about a computational perturbation-response model named **C2S-Scale Perturbation Model**.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel A**: Workflow schematic.\n  - Inputs: **single-cell** data and **bulk RNA** labeled as control inputs.\n  - Model: **C2S-Scale Perturbation Model**, with perturbation sources shown as **drugs**, **cytokines**, and **genetic KOs**; text indicates support for diverse and unseen perturbations.\n  - Interface shows gene inputs/outputs, e.g. **Gene A, Gene B, Gene C**.\n  - Outputs: **Predicted Response** compared with **Ground Truth**.\n  - Evaluation block compares gene-program similarity between predicted and true responses.\n\n- **Panel B**: UMAP/scatter-style embeddings comparing methods.\n  - Columns: **Ground Truth**, **C2S**, **scGen**, **scGPT**, **CellOT**.\n  - Rows include:\n    - **All Unseen Combos**\n    - **CD4 T cells IFNγ Chronic**\n    - **B cells IFNβ IL-6 Chronic**\n  - Points are color-coded:\n    - Yellow: **Untreated GT**\n    - Blue: **Treated GT or model generated**\n    - Gray background points appear as reference cell distribution.\n  - Insets appear in the bottom row for some methods.\n\n- **Panel C**: Bar-chart evaluation for **Cytokine Stimulation**.\n  - Metrics shown:\n    - **scFID ↓**\n    - **MMD ↓**\n    - **Wasserstein-2 Distance ↓**\n  - Methods include **C2S-Scale 410M**, **C2S-Scale 1B**, **scGen**, **CellOT**, **scGPT**, **CPA**, **BioLord**, **Linear Model**, and **Linear Model (100 PCs)**.\n  - C2S bars are highlighted in blue tones and appear lower/better on the plotted error metrics.\n\n- **Panel D**: Bar charts for cytokine stimulation responses.\n  - Left label: **Cytokine Stimulation (Interferon response)**.\n  - Right label: **IL1000 (Apoptosis response)**.\n  - Metrics include **scFID ↓**, **Kendall τ ↑**, and **Pearson r ↑**.\n  - Comparisons show **SFT** versus **GRPO**, with separate groupings for **410M** and **1B** model sizes.\n  - GRPO bars appear improved over SFT for correlation metrics and lower for scFID in the visible plots.\n\n- **Panel E**: Evaluation for **SciPlex**.\n  - Bar charts for:\n    - **scFID ↓**\n    - **MMD ↓**\n    - **Wasserstein ↓**\n  - Methods shown include **C2S-Scale 410M**, **C2S-Scale 1B**, **scGPT**, **chemCPA**, and **CondOT**.\n  - C2S-scale methods have visibly lower scFID and MMD than the other methods in this panel.\n\nBiological/source objects visible: single-cell data, bulk RNA, genes, drugs, cytokines, genetic knockouts, immune cell types including **CD4 T cells** and **B cells**, cytokine perturbations including **IFNγ**, **IFNβ**, and **IL-6**.\n\nMain finding shown visually: the C2S-scale models, especially the **1B** version in several plots, appear to outperform comparison methods on perturbation-response reconstruction metrics such as scFID, MMD, and Wasserstein distance, with reinforcement learning update **GRPO** improving some response metrics over **SFT**."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 35, "caption": "Figure 7: Virtual screening with C2S-Scale nominates a context-dependent modulator of antigen presentation. A, Schematic of the dual-context virtual screening workflow. The model predicts perturbation outcomes on MHC-I antigen presentation gene sets using initial states derived from primary tumor profiles (immune context-positive) and cell line profiles (immune context-neutral). B, Volcano plot displaying predicted drug effects in the primary tumor screen. Silmitasertib (red star) is identified as a high-scoring novel candidate. Inset details the top-ranking compounds, stratified by prior evidence: known MHC-I modulators (dark blue), plausible candidates with pathway links (light blue), and novel hits (grey). C, Experimental validation of the positive control, trametinib. Dose-response assessment of HLA-A,B,C surface levels (Mean Fluorescence Intensity; MFI) in WAGA cells treated with indicated concentrations of trametinib in the absence (gray) or presence (blue) of IFNβ (2 U/ml). D, Predicted context specificity of silmitasertib, showing a significantly higher antigen presentation score in the primary tumor context compared to the cell line context. E, Experimental validation of the novel hit, silmitasertib. Dose-response assessment of MHC-I MFI (normalized to vehicle control) in WAGA cells treated with silmitasertib without (gray) or with (blue) low-dose IFNβ (2 U/ml). Error bars represent mean ± s.d. Significance calculated via two-way t-test with Benjamini-Hochberg correction for multiple comparisons; ∗ P < 0 . 05 , ∗ ∗ P < 0 . 01 , ∗ ∗ ∗ P < 0 . 001 , ∗ ∗ ∗ ∗ P < 0 . 0001 .", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical research figure with panels labeled A-E.\n\nPanel A: Schematic “Virtual Screening Workflow.” Biological source objects are primary tumors marked “immune context-positive” and cell lines marked “immune context-neutral.” These feed into a C2S model/interface and a compound library of 4000+ drugs. Output categories indicate increased MHC-I antigen presentation gene set score as “hits” versus “no effect.”\n\nPanel B: Scatter plot titled “Primary Tumor Screen Results.” Axes show predicted antigen presentation score versus `-log10(p-value)`. Most points are gray/novel, with highlighted reported or plausible drugs in blue and silmitasertib marked with a red star. Labeled compounds include silmitasertib, crizotinib, nutlin-3, trametinib, GSK-126, afatinib, and paclitaxel. A zoomed inset states “16 / 23 Reported or Plausible.”\n\nPanel C: Bar plot for “Trametinib + IFNβ (Known Hit - Positive Control).” Biological measurement is HLA ABC MFI across trametinib concentrations in nM. Gray bars are DMSO only; blue bars are DMSO + 5 U/ml IFNβ. Significance brackets indicate increased HLA ABC MFI, especially with IFNβ.\n\nPanel D: Bar plot titled “Silmitasertib Context Specificity.” It compares predicted antigen presentation score in “Primary Tumor Context” versus “WAGA Cell Line Context,” with higher score in primary tumor context and significance marked.\n\nPanel E: Bar plot for “Silmitasertib + IFNβ (Novel Hit - C2S-predicted).” HLA ABC MFI is plotted across silmitasertib concentrations in nM. Gray bars are DMSO only; blue bars are DMSO + 2 U/ml IFNβ. Results show increased HLA ABC MFI with silmitasertib plus IFNβ, with significance brackets at multiple doses.\n\nOverall finding: the figure presents a C2S-based virtual screening workflow predicting compounds that increase MHC-I antigen presentation, highlights silmitasertib as a context-specific novel hit, and validates increased HLA ABC surface expression with IFNβ combination treatment."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 35, "caption": "Figure 8: C2S allows for conversion from expression information into cell sentence format with minimal information loss. Using a linear model fitted between rank and original expression, cell sentences can be converted back to expression accurately.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains two side-by-side scientific plot panels showing gene expression decay trends by gene log-rank.\n\nPanel 1: Labeled “PBMC”\n- X-axis: “Gene Log-Rank”\n- Y-axis: “Normalized Expression”\n- Blue dense point/line cloud showing normalized expression values decreasing as gene log-rank increases.\n- Red fitted regression line with negative slope.\n- Annotation in upper right: R² = 0.866, Slope = -0.601, Intercept = 2.395.\n- Biological source object: PBMC sample/cell source.\n\nPanel 2: Labeled “Heart”\n- X-axis: “Gene Log-Rank”\n- Y-axis: “Normalized Expression”\n- Blue dense point/line cloud showing normalized expression values decreasing as gene log-rank increases.\n- Red fitted regression line with negative slope.\n- Annotation in upper right: R² = 0.824, Slope = -0.550, Intercept = 2.237.\n- Biological source object: heart tissue/sample source.\n\nVisible transformation/analysis:\n- Genes appear transformed or ordered by log-rank on the x-axis.\n- Expression values are normalized on the y-axis.\n- Linear regression trend lines are fitted to each panel.\n- Reported fit statistics indicate strong negative relationships between gene log-rank and normalized expression in both PBMC and Heart."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 36, "caption": "Figure 9: FLOPs usage by C2S-Scale 2B versus other foundation models and simple model baselines.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nBar chart titled “Total FLOPs Used By Different models.” The y-axis is labeled “Total FLOPs” and uses a logarithmic scale with ticks from about `1e+11` to `1e+23`. The x-axis lists models/classifiers: `C2S-Scale-27B`, `C2S-Scale-2B`, `C2S-Scale-1B`, `C2S-Scale-410M`, `scFoundation`, `scGPT`, `Geneformer`, `scVI`, `Linear Classifier`, `CellTypist`, and `PCA`.\n\nVisible values above bars:\n- C2S-Scale-27B: `5.2e+22`\n- C2S-Scale-2B: `3.2e+21`\n- C2S-Scale-1B: `8.0e+19`\n- C2S-Scale-410M: `2.4e+19`\n- scFoundation: `1.2e+20`\n- scGPT: `7.3e+19`\n- Geneformer: `4.2e+19`\n- scVI: `6.7e+15`\n- Linear Classifier: `4.1e+12`\n- CellTypist: `8.6e+11`\n- PCA: `1.4e+10`\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, or image transformations are shown. The figure compares computational cost/FLOPs across machine learning models and baseline methods, with C2S-Scale-27B using the most FLOPs and PCA the least."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 40, "caption": "Figure 14: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale downstream natural language tasks: cluster captioning and question answering.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel scientific figure showing examples of a language-model style interface for single-cell biology tasks.\n\nTop panel label: “Cluster captioning”\n- Shows a prompt asking to “Describe this single-cell cluster.”\n- Input consists of 5 cells with genes listed in descending order of expression.\n- Visible gene examples include MALAT1, DPP10, RBFOX1, SNHG14, ZNF385D, LRP1B, MEG3, NRXN1, PCDH9, NRG3, DLG2.\n- The response describes a cerebral cortex neuron class with adhesion and signaling programs, mentioning DSCAM, NXPH2, FGFR1, FGF ligands, HTR2C, GRM3/GRM4/GRM8, and GHR.\n- Biological source object: single-cell gene expression profiles from a neuronal cluster.\n- Transformation/interface: raw per-cell ranked gene lists are converted into a natural-language cluster caption.\n\nBottom panel label: “Question answering”\n- Shows a prompt about endothelial cells profiled by single-cell RNA sequencing in diseased tissue.\n- Input lists 10 cells with cell type, tissue, disease status, and ranked gene expression.\n- Visible cell types include fibroblast, endothelial cell, glial cell, pericyte, and myofibroblast cell.\n- Tissues include ileal epithelium, lamina propria of small intestine, small intestine, and ileum.\n- Disease labels include Crohn disease and normal.\n- Visible genes include MALAT1, MT-ATP6, MT-ND3, MT-CO3, MT-CO1, B2M, MT-CO2, MT-CYB, ADAMDEC1, IGFBP7, RGS5, ACTB, MYL9, TPM2, CFD, TMSB4X.\n- The question asks what can be inferred from raw endothelial cell sentences about potential markers of vascular inflammation.\n- The response identifies CD74, IFI27, and HLA-DRA as possible vascular inflammation markers.\n- Biological source object: single-cell RNA-seq-derived cell sentences from intestinal/Crohn disease context.\n- Transformation/interface: structured cell metadata and gene-expression text are used for question answering.\n- Finding shown: the model suggests immune/inflammation-associated endothelial markers, specifically CD74, IFI27, and HLA-DRA."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 43, "caption": "Figure 17: Additional results for spatial tasks. A, Spatial cell generation results. Bars indicate average gene overlap percentage across 100 generated replicates for 100 samples. ** = P < 0.01; two-tailed T-test). B, Niche label prediction results. Dashed line indicates random accuracy. * = P < 0.05, ** = P < 0.01; McNemar's test). Dashed line represents random baseline accuracy (0.184). C, Same niche prediction results. Dashed line indicates random accuracy. * = P < 0.05, ** = P < 0.01; McNemar's test). Dashed line represents random baseline accuracy (0.51). D, Mean and standard deviation of C2S models trained with three different random seeds. Including CPDB and BioGRID interactions consistently improves performance over the base C2S model.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific bar-plot figure with panels labeled **A**, **B**, **C**, and **D**.\n\nPanel **A**: Bar chart titled **“Neighborhood Generation”**. Y-axis is **“Gene overlap %”**. Compares variants labeled approximately **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**. Bars increase slightly from left to right, with a significance bracket marked `**` over the comparison.\n\nPanel **B**: Bar chart titled **“Niche Label Prediction”**. Y-axis is **“Niche Prediction Accuracy”**. Methods include **scGPT**, **Nicheformer**, **C2S**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**, **Linear Model**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**. A dashed horizontal baseline is shown near the lower part of the plot. Multiple significance brackets are shown with `*` and `**`. Highest bars appear for C2S variants and the linear model, with **C2S (w/ BioGrid)** highest.\n\nPanel **C**: Bar chart titled **“Same Niche Prediction”**. Y-axis is **“Same Niche Prediction Accuracy”**. Methods include **GPT-4o**, **Linear Model**, **scGPT**, **C2S**, **Nicheformer**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**. A dashed baseline is shown. C2S variants with biological database inputs perform better, with **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)** highest. Significance brackets marked `*` and `**` are present.\n\nPanel **D**: Three smaller bar charts with error bars:\n- **Neighborhood Prediction**: compares **C2S**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**, and **C2S (w/ CPDB)**; database-augmented versions have higher accuracy.\n- **Niche Label Prediction**: compares **C2S**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**; BioGrid variant appears highest.\n- **Same Niche Prediction**: compares **C2S**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**; BioGrid variant is clearly highest.\n\nBiological/source objects visible in labels: **BioGrid** and **CPDB**, likely biological interaction/pathway database inputs used to augment C2S. No microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecular structures, or model architecture diagrams are visible. The figure presents quantitative model comparison findings for neighborhood generation/prediction and niche prediction tasks."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 44, "caption": "Figure 18: Extended characterization and validation of virtual screening candidates in tumor fragment and cell line models. A, Experimental validation of top hits in an immune-context-positive tumor fragment model. Surface levels of HLA-A,B,C (Mean Fluorescence Intensity; MFI) were measured following treatment with 10 nM of each drug. IFNγ serves as a positive control. Significance is compared to DMSO vehicle. B, Computational characterization of the immune-context-neutral state. (Top Left) Baseline Type I interferon (IFN) response gene signature scores for the Primary Tumor context compared to three cell lines (WAGA, UTSCC45, MCC2). (Top Right, Bottom) Volcano plots displaying predicted drug effects in these cell line contexts. Silmitasertib (red marker) shows no significant predicted efficacy in these interferon-low settings compared to the primary tumor context. C, Validation of the interferon-conditional effect in MDK-knockout WAGA cells. Dose-response assessment of HLA-A,B,C MFI in MDK-knockout WAGA cells treated with silmitasertib in the absence (gray) or presence (blue) of low-dose IFNβ (2 U/ml). D, Generalization of the amplification effect to Type II interferon. Dose-response assessment of HLA-A,B,C MFI in MDK-knockout WAGA cells treated with silmitasertib in the absence (gray) or presence (blue) of low-dose IFNγ (2 U/ml). Error bars represent mean ± s.d. Significance calculated via two-way t-test with Benjamini-Hochberg correction; ∗ P < 0 . 05 , ∗ ∗ P < 0 . 01 , ∗ ∗ ∗ P < 0 . 001 , ∗ ∗ ∗ ∗ P < 0 . 0001 .", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure with panels A-D.\n\nPanel A: Bar plot titled “Immune Context-Positive Tumor Fragment Model.” Y-axis is “HLA-A/B/C MFI.” Conditions on x-axis: DMSO, Silmitasertib, Afatinib, GSK-126, Crizotinib, Trametinib, IFN-γ. Bars show increased HLA-A/B/C MFI for several treatments, with highest values for IFN-γ and strong increases for Crizotinib/Trametinib. Individual replicate points and significance brackets are shown, including ns p=0.07, *, **, ***, and ****.\n\nPanel B: Computational/model-related panel. Left subpanel is a bar chart titled “Baseline IFN Response,” showing “Baseline IFN-Response Gene Signature Score” for Primary Tumor, WAGA, UTSCC45, and MCC2. Right and lower subpanels are volcano/scatter plots for WAGA, UTSCC45, and MCC2. X-axis is “Predicted Antigen Presentation Score,” y-axis is “-log10(p-value).” Points are colored by treatment labels CX-4945, DMSO, and trametinib, with dashed red p=0.05 threshold lines. This appears to show predicted antigen-presentation effects across cell models/treatments.\n\nPanel C: Bar plot titled “Silmitasertib + IFNβ, WAGA MDK KO.” Y-axis is “HLA ABC MFI.” X-axis is “[Silmitasertib] nM” with doses 0, 30, 100, 300, 1000 under DMSO-only and DMSO + 2 U/ml IFNβ conditions. Biological source/object: WAGA MDK knockout cells. IFNβ plus silmitasertib increases HLA ABC MFI relative to DMSO-only, with dose-associated increases and significance brackets.\n\nPanel D: Bar plot titled “Silmitasertib + IFNγ, WAGA MDK KO.” Y-axis is “HLA ABC MFI.” X-axis is “[Silmitasertib] nM” with doses 0, 10, 30, 100, 300 under DMSO-only and DMSO + 2 U/ml IFNγ conditions. Biological source/object: WAGA MDK knockout cells. IFNγ produces a strong increase in HLA ABC MFI, with additional increases at silmitasertib doses and significance annotations.\n\nOverall visible finding: kinase inhibitor treatments, especially silmitasertib in combination with interferon stimulation, are associated with increased HLA class I / HLA ABC surface expression, and model-derived antigen presentation scores are compared across WAGA, UTSCC45, and MCC2 contexts."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 45, "caption": "Figure 19: A, Diagram of the scFID metric, computed in foundation model latent space, analogous to FID in computer vision. B, UMAP showing the 10 most frequent cell types in the Dominguez immune dataset [20]. C, The scFID is significantly lower within cells of the same cell type than between different cell types (two sample t-test; 10 samples within cell types, 45 samples between cell types; P = 2 . 23 × 10 -4 ). D, Confusion matrix showing the scFID between every pair of cell types. E-G Same as B-D, but for Leiden clusters instead of cell (two sample t-test; 18 samples within clusters, 153 samples between clusters; P = 2 . 60 × 10 -9 .)", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-G about evaluating single-cell foundation model embeddings with scFID.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. Microscopy-like biological image patches or tissue/cell regions are input to a “Foundation Model.” The model produces embedding distributions across embedding dimensions for two groups shown in blue and red. Each distribution is summarized by mean μ and covariance Σ, then compared using Wasserstein distance to compute scFID.\n\nPanel B: UMAP plot titled “Cell Type Clustering - UMAP.” Points are colored by annotated immune cell types. Axes are UMAP 1 and UMAP 2. The legend includes cell-type categories such as naive thymus-derived CD4-positive cells, memory B cells, CD8-positive alpha-beta memory cells, classical monocytes, CD16-positive/CD56-dim natural killer cells, effector memory CD4-positive cells, alveolar macrophages, CD4-positive helper T cells, T follicular helper cells, and effector memory CD8-positive cells.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot titled “Cell Type - scFID Comparison.” It compares scFID for Inter versus Within cell-type groups. Inter values are higher than Within values, with a significance marker shown above the comparison.\n\nPanel D: Heatmap titled “Cell Type - scFID Matrix.” Rows and columns are the same immune cell-type labels. The matrix shows pairwise scFID values, with darker/lighter blue indicating magnitude according to a colorbar.\n\nPanel E: UMAP plot titled “Leiden Clustering (res=0.5) - UMAP.” Points are colored by Leiden clusters labeled Cluster 0 through Cluster 17. Axes are UMAP 1 and UMAP 2.\n\nPanel F: Boxplot titled “Leiden - scFID Comparison.” It compares Inter versus Within scFID for Leiden clusters. Inter-cluster scFID is higher than within-cluster scFID, with a significance marker.\n\nPanel G: Heatmap titled “Leiden - scFID Matrix.” Pairwise scFID values are shown between Leiden clusters 0-17, with a blue color scale and numeric values in cells.\n\nVisible finding: scFID separates between-group comparisons from within-group comparisons for both annotated cell types and Leiden clusters, with inter-group distances generally larger than within-group distances."]}]

